From: ReArc Authors
Subject: [PATCH] P-TAB-001 screen sharing: getDisplayMedia asks the host what to share (ABI 1.70)

The owner, in a Google Meet call on Darni 2026.1123.1255: "Present now"
answers "Can't share your screen. Something went wrong when screen sharing."

Why. navigator.mediaDevices.getDisplayMedia() reaches the tab's
WebContentsDelegate as a media access request of the display types
(DISPLAY_VIDEO_CAPTURE, with DISPLAY_AUDIO_CAPTURE when the page asks for
sound). ReArcTabDelegate::RequestMediaAccessPermission (tab/038: "keep
screen capture unsupported") gave everything but camera and microphone to
content::WebContentsDelegate, whose answer is NOT_SUPPORTED, at once. No
picker, no permission of macOS, nothing in TCC: the unified log of the
failed attempt has camera and microphone requests only.

What Chrome does. MediaCaptureDevicesDispatcher gives the request to
DisplayMediaAccessHandler. The handler checks the display-capture
permissions policy, the user gesture and that the page is visible, queues
the request per tab, and asks a DesktopMediaPicker: a Views dialog on the
browser window with tabs (TabDesktopMediaList, which walks Browser
windows), windows and screens. On macOS 15 and later the last two are not
lists: with UseSCContentSharingPicker the dialog hands over to the picker
of macOS (SCContentSharingPicker), and what the person points at there
needs no Screen Recording permission. While a share is live Chrome shows a
tab-sharing infobar or an "is sharing your screen" window, both Views.

What this patch does. The handler stays Chromium's, with its checks, its
queue and its devices; its two pieces of UI become the host's.

- rearc::MakeDisplayMediaAccessHandler (rearc_display_capture.cc) builds
  the handler for the dispatcher: under the shell, with HostPickerFactory
  and display_notification false; in a process without the shell, as
  upstream. One line of media_capture_devices_dispatcher.cc calls it.
- HostPicker is the DesktopMediaPicker. Show sends the host one JSON event
  (ReArcEngineSetDisplayCaptureCallback, phase 0): who asks, whether sound
  was asked for, which kinds may be offered. The host draws its own sheet
  and answers with ReArcDisplayCaptureRespond: cancel, a tab of its own, a
  window or the screen. The factory makes no source lists: the host knows
  its tabs, and the engine never lists windows or draws their thumbnails.
- A tab: the engine checks that it is a live tab of the asking tab's
  profile and browser context (and not the asking tab when the page
  excluded itself) and answers with its WebContentsMediaCaptureId, as
  Chromium's picker does for a row of its tab list. It must also be a
  page (IsShareablePage, security review SS-02 of
  security/PRE-screen-sharing.md): contents with the "tab contents" view
  type that are no hosted DevTools frontend or toolbox, whose main frame
  is on an http, https or file address. A DevTools frontend shows the
  inspected page's storage, cookies and network data; it, an extension's
  popup or side panel and every browser page are refused with
  invalid_argument, whatever the host's sheet listed.
- A window or the screen: the engine opens the picker of macOS with
  content::desktop_capture::OpenNativeScreenCapturePicker, the call
  DelegatedSourceListCapturer makes for Chrome's dialog, and answers with
  the picker's source. The handler's own rule stays: below macOS 15 it
  refuses such a choice without the Screen Recording permission, so the
  request's JSON says whether the permission is needed and missing, and the
  host explains and sends the person to System Settings instead of opening
  a picker whose choice would be refused.
- Every request the host was asked ends with one phase 1 event (shared,
  cancelled, failed, withdrawn), so its sheet never outlives the request.
  Nothing follows tab.closed. A new or cleared callback refuses what the
  old host was asked.
- The live share is already in media_capture (capturing_display, ABI
  1.46). ReArcTabStopDisplayCapture ends a tab's shares through
  MediaStreamCaptureIndicator::StopMediaCapturing(kDisplayMedia), the call
  behind the Stop button of Chromium's own UI.
- ReArcTabDelegate gives the dispatcher the requests the handler is for
  (rearc::IsHostDisplayCaptureRequest: video of a tab, window or screen,
  alone or with its sound). Audio alone, an extension's desktopCapture and
  tabCapture streams and getAllScreensMedia stay refused, as before.
  CheckMediaAccessPermission is unchanged.

Who gets a sheet. Only a request from an http or https frame of a registry
tab, and only while a host callback is set; anything else is refused with
NOT_SUPPORTED, as before the patch. Chromium's rules still run first: no
gesture, a page nobody sees (CAPTURE_FROM_BACKGROUND_PAGE_ON_MAC) or a
frame without the display-capture permissions policy never reach the
picker.

Sound. A tab's sound is Chromium's own loopback of that tab. The sound of
the Mac (with the screen, or with a window when the page asks for it) is a
CoreAudio tap (macOS 14.2 and later): macOS asks the person itself the
first time, and needs NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription in the app's
Info.plist, which shell/scripts/bundle-chromium.sh now writes. The JSON
says for each kind whether sound can go with it; the host decides whether
to offer it.

The feature. UseSCContentSharingPicker is off by default in Chromium and
on in the field trial testing configuration, which ReArc engines keep
(build-engine-release.sh). Every build since 500 has run with it: the log
of the owner's call has -[SCContentSharingPicker setActive:] from
VideoCaptureManager::DestroyControllerIfNoClients, which closes the native
picker's session for any video device that stops, the camera included.
rearc_engine_abi.mm now names the feature in --enable-features, so the
picker does not depend on that configuration. The set of features that are
on does not change, and nothing is written to a profile.

State. Nothing is persisted: no preference, no content setting, no
permission remembered. Chromium keeps no grant for getDisplayMedia; every
request asks again.

ABI 1.70, bridge 0.81.0, feature "display_capture". Three functions, JSON
over scalars, no struct added or changed.

Type-checked with the tree's clang (out/ReArc-codecs-arm64 flags,
Chromium's plugins, -fsyntax-only) against overlay c7000854cf, then built
in out/ReArc-codecs-arm64 (overlay fd500c7ffb, 2026-11-23; with SS-02 as
a second overlay commit on it, see engine/patches/series): the framework
exports the three functions. Not run: the self-test step ("screen
sharing", ChromiumSelfTest.screenShareChecks) needs the screen.

Touches: chrome/browser/media/webrtc/media_capture_devices_dispatcher.cc
(first ReArc hunk in that file), chrome/browser/BUILD.gn, chrome/app/
framework.{exports,order}, and the bridge's tab_delegate.cc,
rearc_engine_abi.{h,mm}, rearc_engine_entry_mac.mm, rearc_abi_internal.h,
rearc_services_abi.cc. New: rearc_display_capture.{cc,h}.

Last rebased: 2026-11-23 onto Chromium 156.0.8078.109 (overlay c7000854cf).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>

